<Project>

  <!--
    Shared MSBuild properties for every project of the solution. MSBuild imports
    this file automatically before each project body; a project can still
    override any value locally. Only properties that are genuinely common
    belong here — target frameworks stay in the csproj files (the backend
    targets net10.0 while the Blazor hosts and generated clients target
    net9.0), and per-project concerns (RunPostBuildEvent…) stay local.
    https://learn.microsoft.com/visualstudio/msbuild/customize-by-directory
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What makes `.editorconfig` a ruleset rather than a list of preferences. Seventy-three rules
    were declared there at `warning` severity while nothing turned a warning into a failure, so
    every one of them was emitted and scrolled past — ADR 0017 recorded exactly that and left it.
    See ADR 0019 for the census that made turning this on safe, and for why each carve-out exists.

    The properties rather than `dotnet build -warnaserror`: the CLI switch also promotes what the
    MSBuild engine emits — assembly-version conflicts, restore-graph noise — none of which is code
    quality, and any of which would fail a build for a reason nobody chose.

    EnforceCodeStyleInBuild is the half that was never running: without it the IDE* rules exist in
    an editor and nowhere else.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>

    <!--
      IDE0005 (unnecessary usings) rides on the compiler's unused-using detection, which is off
      unless documentation generation is on. Measured, not assumed: zero occurrences without this,
      twenty-eight with.

      It also turns on CS1591, "missing XML comment on a publicly visible type or member", on nine
      hundred and eleven members at the time. ADR 0019 silenced it and ADR 0020 wrote them instead,
      so nothing exempts it now — and the document ASP.NET Core publishes carries the result.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <!--
      AnalysisLevel follows the target framework, so the three net9.0 projects would analyze at a
      different level from the other twenty-three and the ruleset would quietly differ across one
      solution. Pinned after measuring that aligning them costs nothing.
    -->
    <AnalysisLevel>10.0</AnalysisLevel>

    <!--
      The .NET analyzers ship around four hundred and fifty CA rules and enable a fraction of them
      by default. `.editorconfig` names forty-nine more, all hand-picked, with nothing recorded
      about where the list came from. Switching every category on at once (`AnalysisMode=All`)
      produces one thousand and thirty warnings — of which five hundred and forty-seven are
      CA1707, "identifiers should not contain underscores", against test names this repository
      writes as sentences on purpose, and one hundred and forty-four are CA2007, ConfigureAwait,
      in an application that has no synchronization context to return to.

      So: by category, and only the categories that measured cheap. These three are free — the
      census found not one occurrence in any of them, which is worth more than it sounds. Security
      is the whole CA2100/CA3xxx/CA5xxx family, in a codebase holding a JWT issuer, a reverse
      proxy that attaches tokens server-side, and EF Core; that it reports nothing today is the
      reason to keep it reporting.
    -->
    <AnalysisModeSecurity>All</AnalysisModeSecurity>
    <AnalysisModeDocumentation>All</AnalysisModeDocumentation>
    <AnalysisModeInteroperability>All</AnalysisModeInteroperability>

    <!--
      Globalization costs four: CA1307 three times and CA1308 once, all four fixed here. Kept
      because CA1305, its sibling, has been enforced all along — a ruleset that demands an
      IFormatProvider on formatting and says nothing about StringComparison on comparison is
      enforcing half a rule.

      Maintainability is the category that was measured and left out. Its only two findings were
      CA1515 sixty-three times — make an application's public types internal, which would need
      InternalsVisibleTo plumbing to keep `WebApplicationFactory<Program>`, MVC's controller
      discovery and the shared test kit compiling — and CA1508 four times, all four in equality
      tests that assert at run time what the analyzer proves at compile time. A category switch
      whose every consequence is turned down reads like enforcement and does nothing, so CA1508 is
      declared on its own in .editorconfig, under src/ where it costs nothing and means something.
    -->
    <AnalysisModeGlobalization>All</AnalysisModeGlobalization>

    <!--
      NuGet audit findings stay loud and stop blocking. They are not rules this repository chose
      and then ignored — they are security advisories about the dependency graph, and promoting
      them means a CVE published against any transitive package turns all three workflows red with
      nobody having pushed anything. Directory.Packages.props already pins one package to close a
      GHSA, so this is a live hazard rather than a theoretical one.
    -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;NU1605</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
